  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1000 AM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nA tranquil rest of the holiday is expected today. Highs are\nstill expected to peak above normal. Going with highs in the 66F\nto 70F range this afternoon. A light northwest wind is expected\nfor the rest of the day and into the evening hours. The tranquil\nweather pattern will continue through the overnight hours. Lows\nare expected to be above freezing and will range from 35F to\n40F.\n\nA backdoor cold front will move across a portion of the \nforecast area Thursday. There will be a fairly large range of \nhighs from the mid 50s across central Kansas to around 70F by \nElkhart. No moisture is expected with front. The main impacts \nwill be a subtle wind shift and the mentioned temperature range.\nContinued low level cold air advection will continue Friday \nmorning. As a result, a light freeze will be possible across the\neastern zones. Does not look like a hard freeze at this time.\n\nWe will be in between weather systems Friday and Saturday. A\nfairly uneventful weather pattern is expected during this\nperiod. Temperatures will trend to near normal for the end of\nthe week and into the beginning of the weekend. Attention then\nturns to Sunday. A large upper level low is expected to traverse\nacross the Great Plains Sunday. Moisture advection will occur\nahead of this upper level low. Forecast PWATs are expected to\nincrease in the 0.50\" to 1.00\" range. This moisture and the\nupper level low will set the stage for rain showers Sunday.\nThe models are not forecasting any CAPE Sunday, so thunderstorms\nare not expected. Precipitation amounts are still uncertain. \nThere is considerable spread in both the GEFS and ENS. The \nofficial forecast as of right now has about 0.10\" in the north \nto as much as 0.50\" across the Oklahoma border.\n\nBeyond Sunday, the ensembles are indicating shortwave ridging\ndeveloping across the Great Plains Monday and Tuesday. This \nwould support the notion of drier and warmer forecast. \nTemperatures might even be a few degrees above normal by the \nmiddle portion of the next business week.",
